      "text": "(note) This problem appears on pp. 181--182 of \"Mock AMC\" solutions tab (this is usually the forum to ask for SolMan assistance). So, when someone posts a problem it's considered good policy to paste in all of your work, including any guesses, etc. :)\n\nYou can't just pick 9x8x7...x1 in 9!. That would be the same as saying there are 10 circular permutations of nine white balls among themselves too, so why not?  :lol: Fix one of the people. You are left with 8 seats, of which 7 are distinguishable, thus the number of circular permutations is $ 8\\cdot 7 \\equal{} 56$.\n\nCircles? Why does it always have to be a circle? WLOG fix a person and rest is circular permutations, or does that only apply to non-rotations?\n\nEither there's only 1 person or 9 persons seated. Obviously the former is unnecessary for your question. And to fix one person is not circular permutations.\r\n\r\nSo in your case two are seated (one must continue where you left them). That is 1 way to seat your remaining 8 people. If you were to have the 9 folks, I think there would be $\\boxed{8}$ ways to rotate 1 person (that would come out of $ 9\\choose1\\)$.\r\n\r\nMais je tiens le forum... hahaha\n\nIt's a typo. Sorry for that.\r\n\r\n$ \\boxed{8}$ :P  :(\n\nThanks, all of you. With the \"Um, mobile phones convergence between the United States and Canada\" I was blind, I really do apologize and thank you for your corrections.\n\nIn my solution, I think 10 would be far less than 10! Basically there are 10 possible seatings of 8, which are identical to the circle, so I get 8, which is a more accurate answer. :rotfl:\n\nIn my rush this evening, I forgot which of the first 10 permutations were phase related and which were not.  So when I rescheduled my full round, my mistake for the rotation was not a typo -- it did happen.\n\nFrom the revolving door of AMC Olymat, your mistakes are rewarded, man, so dusty and round with a proper back.\n\nYes! $\\boxed8$\n\nFixed in the OC (contact me 